cartridge "BOMBS AWAY!/J.FRALEIGH/1988"

' BOMBS AWAY!
' A ColecoVision game based on "Bombs Away!" by John Fraleigh, Ahoy!
' October 1988 (Commodore 64 machine language, BOMBS AWAY.ML at $C000,
' started by a two-line BASIC loader). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an
' AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/bombs-ml.dis), the game is built for the
' ColecoVision: a brick city wall at dusk, a hooded firebug walking on top
' as a smooth two-colour sprite, round bombs and dynamite with sputtering
' fuses, three wicker baskets, a blast where a bomb lands, sound effects.
'
' The game: a mad arsonist walks along the top of a wall and drops bombs.
' Catch every one in your baskets. A bomb that reaches the street blows up
' and takes your bottom basket; the third one ends the game. Every 100
' bombs caught the level goes up: the bombs fall faster, the firebug walks
' faster and each catch is worth 10 more.
'
' From the original (orig/): the step loop that moves every bomb and the
' bomber one pixel; its delay 245 - 10 x level (CLRCHN calls of 44 cycles,
' worked out into pixels per frame for the 60 Hz C64: 1.44 at level 1,
' SpdT); a new bomb every 41 steps under the bomber's middle, into the next
' of five slots in turn; the bomber turns left or right at random at the
' same moment and stays between X 28 and 242; the catcher moves 2 pixels a
' frame between X 28 and 255; a bomb lower than Y 188 that touches the
' baskets is caught; one that reaches Y 230 explodes: everything stops
' for 26 jiffies while the catcher can still move, then the baskets drop by
' one (the lowest goes); three misses end the game; 5 + 10 x level points a
' catch (15 at level 1), 100 catches a level, round bombs and dynamite
' taking turns every 4 levels; the score and the high score (six digits;
' here they stop at 999999).
'
' Liberties: the whole field is scaled 7/8 (the C64's 263 x 200 to the
' ColecoVision's 230 x 175) while speeds and every rule stay in the C64's
' pixels; collisions are box tests on the bomb's and baskets' drawn areas
' (the C64 used its sprite collision register); from level 9 the C64's
' firebug walked faster than its catcher: here, from level 6 on, the
' catcher moves 9/8 as fast
' as the bombs fall so the game stays playable; the speed stops rising at
' level 16 (the C64's delay ran out at level 24 and wrapped round to slow
' at 25) and a catch is worth at most 255; the stick's diagonals move the
' catcher (the C64 read only plain left and right); a sound for every catch
' and a jingle for a new level (the C64 had the drop whistle and the blast);
' the fuses sputter by changing shape three times a second (the C64 swapped
' the bombs' colours every frame); GAME OVER then the title (the C64 asked
' TO PLAY AGAIN PRESS TRIGGER).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick moves the baskets left and
' right. On the title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given (bug
' 25). Own xorshift random numbers (bug 36).

